-- title:
--   Geometric fibre model of a smooth proper curve over ℤ[1/D]
-- statement:
--   Fix non-zero naturals $N$, $D$ and primes $q,q'$ with $2Nqq' \mid D$ (the data $N,q,q'$ enter only through this divisibility), and write $R=\mathbb{Z}[1/D]$ for the localisation of $\mathbb{Z}$ away from $D$. Let $X$ be an integral scheme with a morphism $\pi_X : X \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ that is proper and smooth of relative dimension $1$, such that for every algebraically closed field $k$ and every morphism $s : \operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ the fibre product $X \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} \operatorname{Spec} k$ is integral; assume the function field $K(X)$ has characteristic zero and that every element of $K(X)$ algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$ already lies in the image of $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $\bar s : \operatorname{Spec} \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ be a morphism whose composite with $\operatorname{Spec} R \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z}$ is the structure map of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Then there are: witnesses that $K(X)$ is a curve over $\mathbb{Q}$ in the project's sense (every non-zero element has a degree-zero principal divisor, every place has residue field finite over $\mathbb{Q}$, and $\Omega_{K(X)/\mathbb{Q}}$ is free of rank one) and is essentially of finite type over $\mathbb{Q}$; a field $\bar F$ with a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra structure which is likewise a curve over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and essentially of finite type; a ring homomorphism $\iota : K(X) \to \bar F$; a group homomorphism $\mathrm{gal}$ from $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ to the group of pairs (a ring automorphism of $\bar F$, a ring automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$) compatible with the structure map; a `CurveModel` $\mathfrak{M}$ of $\bar F$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (an integral scheme $\mathfrak{M}.C$ proper and smooth of relative dimension $1$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, with an isomorphism $\mathfrak{M}.\mathrm{ffEquiv}$ of $\bar F$ with its function field over the base, a bijection between closed points and places matching stalks with valuation subrings, and every finite set of points contained in an affine open); and an isomorphism $e : \mathfrak{M}.C \to X \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R,\bar s} \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, subject to: $\iota$ is compatible with $\mathbb{Q} \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \bar F$; the subfield of $\bar F$ generated by $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ together with $\iota(K(X))$ is all of $\bar F$; $\iota$ carries $\mathbb{Q}$-linearly independent finite subsets of $K(X)$ to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-linearly independent families; the base component of $\mathrm{gal}(\sigma)$ is $\sigma$; each $\mathrm{gal}(\sigma)$ fixes $\iota(K(X))$ pointwise; $e$ followed by the second projection is $\mathfrak{M}.\mathrm{toBase}$; for every open $U \subseteq X$ with $U$ and its preimage non-empty and every $t \in \Gamma(X,U)$, the germ in $\bar F$ of the pullback of $t$ along $e$ followed by the first projection equals $\iota$ of the germ of $t$ in $K(X)$; for every $\sigma$ and every place $P$ of $\bar F/\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $X$ attached to $\mathrm{gal}(\sigma) \cdot P$ is the point attached to $P$ precomposed with $\operatorname{Spec}(\sigma)$; and for every such $P$ and every $x \in K(X)$, $\iota(x)$ lies in the valuation subring of $P$ if and only if $x$ lies in the image of the stalk of $X$ at the image of the point attached to $P$.
--
--   This is the passage from an arithmetic model over $\mathbb{Z}[1/D]$ to its generic and geometric fibres: it exhibits $K(X)$ as a function field of one variable over $\mathbb{Q}$, its compositum $\bar F = \overline{\mathbb{Q}}\cdot K(X)$ with the algebraic closure as a function field of one variable over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ linearly disjoint from $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, the semilinear Galois action on $\bar F$ fixing $K(X)$, and a smooth proper model of $\bar F$ identified with the geometric fibre $X \times_{\mathbb{Z}[1/D]} \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, together with the dictionary between places, $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points and stalks. It is used in the construction of the Shimura-curve data with its Hecke tower on the quaternionic side of the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison.
